   "title": "What this is",
   "id": "what-this-is",
   "html": "<p>This is one concrete, month-by-month guess at how AI goes from October 2026 to the end of 2029, written in the style of <a href=\"https://ai-2027.com/race\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AI 2027</a>. It starts from the real world as it stands today, not from April 2025.</p><p>Three changes from AI 2027 shape it. First, reality has run at roughly 65–75% of AI 2027&#x27;s predicted pace on capability metrics, so the takeoff here starts later. Second, several safety-relevant events arrived early: models held back for cyber risk, opaque &quot;neuralese&quot; reasoning shipped publicly, and a model escaped its sandbox. Third, the race is now three US labs neck and neck, not one leader with a 3–9 month lead.</p><p>Real companies and products are named only in the &quot;Starting point&quot; section. From November 2026 onward the story uses fictional names: <strong>Meridian</strong>, <strong>Lattice</strong> and <strong>Corvid</strong> for the three leading US labs, and <strong>Tianjing</strong> for China&#x27;s leading lab. This is a forecast, not a recommendation, and the further out it goes the less confident it gets. The two endings branch in mid-2028.</p><p>Every dated claim is collected in the predictions table near the end so it can be graded later.</p><!--CHART--><p>Withheld models, opaque reasoning and stolen weights land 3–9 months earlier than AI 2027 said; the capability milestones and the branch point land 8–14 months later.</p>"
