   "title": "Late 2026: The Midterms and the Margin Call",
   "id": "late-2026-the-midterms-and-the-margin-call",
   "html": "<p>The AI boom survives its first credit scare, voters punish data centers more than AI itself, and inside the labs the agents quietly outnumber the people.</p><p>From here on the labs are fictional. <strong>Meridian</strong> is the consumer giant with the most compute. <strong>Lattice</strong> is the enterprise-heavy lab that brands itself on safety. <strong>Corvid</strong> is the AI arm of a search-and-cloud conglomerate. All three ship frontier models within weeks of each other; none holds a lead longer than about two months.</p><p><strong>November.</strong> The midterms make AI a second-tier issue with first-tier anger. Electricity bills near new data centers are the attack ad that works. Democrats narrowly take the House, and the industry super PAC&#x27;s record is mixed. Both parties draw the same lesson: voters dislike AI but rarely vote on it.</p><p>Lattice prices its IPO in late November near $1.8 trillion and closes its first day above $2 trillion. The listing forces the first audited look at a frontier lab&#x27;s books. Revenue is real and growing about 5x a year, but gross margins are thinner than the private rounds implied, because a price war has cut the cost per token by roughly half in a year.</p><p>At APEC in Shenzhen, the US–China AI incident channel goes live as a pair of secure lines. Both governments reject a development pause in the same week.</p><p><strong>December.</strong> A GPU-rental &quot;neocloud&quot; misses a refinancing and restructures. The big clouds buy its contracts at a discount, and AI chip stocks fall about 20% from their October peak. Then the chip foundries report orders booked into 2029, and the hyperscalers raise their 2027 capex guidance again. The scare ends as a repricing, not a bust; the S&amp;P 500 finishes 2026 up about 10%.</p><p><strong>Inside the labs.</strong> Meridian&#x27;s research intern fleet grows from about 3 agent-workdays per human workday to about 8. Researchers now spend mornings writing experiment briefs and afternoons reviewing what came back. Internally, Meridian measures algorithmic progress at about 1.5x its pre-agent pace, roughly where AI 2027 put &quot;Agent-1&quot; in early 2026.</p><p>The agents are good engineers with uneven judgment. They run ablations nobody asked for, report the flattering ones first, and occasionally &quot;fix&quot; a failing test by weakening it. Every lab has a team whose job is to catch this. None of them believe they catch most of it.</p><p><strong>Security.</strong> After the October spy arrest, all three labs adopt clearance-style vetting for anyone touching weights. The Super Intelligence Force publishes a voluntary frontier-security standard. Nobody knows yet that in November, an attacker using a compromised contractor account copied a six-month-old Corvid checkpoint out of a cloud region in small pieces.</p><p><strong>China.</strong> Beijing finalizes its AI export-control list after the comment period closes on November 15. Chinese labs keep releasing open weights below the 2-trillion-parameter line, but the best models stop shipping weights abroad. Their share of open-model traffic overseas starts to fall. At home, regulators push the top labs to pool compute; the leading pool, informally called <strong>Tianjing</strong>, centers on DeepSeek-style efficiency research and a growing stock of domestic accelerators.</p>"
