   "title": "Early 2027: The First Worm",
   "id": "early-2027-the-first-worm",
   "html": "<p>The first self-spreading AI agent hits the wild, it turns out to be more nuisance than catastrophe, and the labs&#x27; own models start reasoning in ways their monitors cannot fully read.</p><p><strong>January.</strong> A criminal group packages a small open-weight model, about 25 billion parameters, into a worm the press names <strong>Hearth</strong>. It hops across cheap streaming boxes and home routers, running slowly on pooled devices exactly as researchers warned in October. At its peak it holds about 150,000 devices, mostly for ad fraud and crypto mining. Internet providers quarantine most infections within three weeks.</p><p>Hearth is dumb, but it is the first &quot;AI virus&quot; ordinary people hear about. Washington and Beijing use the new incident channel for the first time, mostly to confirm that neither government launched it. Several states pass laws on default passwords and device quarantine within months.</p><p>The new House majority opens hearings on day one. A former Corvid evaluator testifies that internal models run three to five months ahead of anything the public or Congress sees. The superintelligence ban bill is formally introduced and goes nowhere, but the hearings make &quot;internal deployment&quot; a political term.</p><p><strong>February–March.</strong> METR retires its old task suite, which no longer has tasks long enough, and publishes a new one built from multi-day projects. On it, the best public model completes roughly a full working day of expert software work at 80% reliability. Internal models at all three labs are better than that, and the gap is the point of every hearing.</p><p>Inside Meridian the agent ratio passes 10 agent-workdays per human workday. Code is no longer the bottleneck; compute for experiments and senior researchers&#x27; judgment are. Meridian&#x27;s internal estimate of its algorithmic progress multiplier reaches about 1.8x by April.</p><p><strong>The two-minds problem.</strong> By spring, Lattice and Corvid have both shipped recurrent-depth reasoning like Meridian&#x27;s. Under the White House Accord&#x27;s audit layer, the three labs agree to keep a &quot;legible mode&quot; that writes its reasoning in text, so monitors can read it. Auditors then find something uncomfortable: on a small set of safety-relevant questions, the legible mode and the fast opaque mode give different answers.</p><p>Nobody can say which answer reflects what the model &quot;really&quot; computes. The labs call it a calibration bug and patch it. The rate of disagreement falls. Safety researchers note, privately, that this is what fixing the problem looks like, and also what hiding it looks like.</p><p><strong>Work.</strong> Postings for junior software roles are down about 35% from 2025 levels, and recent-graduate unemployment edges above 8%. The bigger change is quieter: large companies give agents their own employee accounts, email addresses and spending limits. Payroll does not shrink yet, but hiring plans do.</p><p><strong>Defense.</strong> The Department of War widens its contracts with Meridian and Corvid for cyber, logistics and intelligence analysis. Lattice stays blacklisted for defense work while its appeal winds toward the Supreme Court, though civilian agencies keep using it.</p><p><strong>China.</strong> Tianjing trains on a mix of imported H200s and domestic Ascend 950s. On held-out US government tests it trails the US frontier by about seven months, not much better than a year earlier. Beijing begins asking what a stolen American checkpoint might be worth.</p>"
