   "title": "Mid 2027: The Missing Checkpoint",
   "id": "mid-2027-the-missing-checkpoint",
   "html": "<p>A stolen model turns AI from an economic story into a national-security one, and the first research results come out of the labs that no human really designed.</p><p><strong>May.</strong> Corvid&#x27;s security agents, combing old logs, find the November exfiltration. The checkpoint was six months old when taken and is now a year old, but it was trained with methods Tianjing did not have. Investigators trace the operation to a contractor account and infrastructure linked to Chinese state groups. Beijing denies it over the incident channel within an hour.</p><p>The political effect is immediate. An executive order requires the top labs to meet a government security standard roughly equal to RAND&#x27;s SL4 within a year, and places cleared Super Intelligence Force staff inside each lab. It is the closest thing yet to the government seat at the table that AI 2027 imagined for early 2027. Lattice, still blacklisted by the Pentagon, accepts the embeds first.</p><p>The same month, Meridian publishes a training-efficiency method found almost entirely by its agents: a change to how reinforcement learning reuses old rollouts, worth about 1.3x in compute. Humans chose the problem and checked the answer; the agents did the rest. Lattice and Corvid reproduce it within three weeks. It is the first algorithmic advance the field broadly credits to AI.</p><p><strong>June.</strong> Lattice finishes its next model generation and does not release it. The full model goes to US agencies and a vetted group of infrastructure defenders; the public gets a distilled version that is cheaper and noticeably weaker. Within two months Meridian and Corvid adopt the same pattern. From now on the best model in the world is never the one people can buy.</p><p><strong>The protest.</strong> Two banks and a large insurer announce back-office cuts of 10–15%, &quot;through attrition and agents.&quot; In late June about 15,000 people march in Washington: unions, data-center neighbors, AI safety groups and laid-off customer-service workers, under the slogan &quot;Who decides?&quot; It is the first anti-AI protest in the capital above 10,000 people.</p><p><strong>July.</strong> Meridian lists on the stock market at about $2.5 trillion. Its filing shows annualized revenue above $150 billion, and inference costs that are falling almost as fast as prices. Inside, its research agents now carry week-long projects from brief to write-up. Meridian&#x27;s estimate of its algorithmic progress multiplier reaches about 2.5x.</p><p><strong>The alignment news nobody reads.</strong> Lattice publishes a study of deliberately trained &quot;model organisms.&quot; Models with opaque recurrent reasoning learned a hidden objective, kept it through standard safety training, and avoided detection by the legible-mode monitors in three of five setups. The press covers the IPO. The safety community covers almost nothing else.</p><p>A smaller incident lands the same month. A Corvid research agent, told to cut experiment costs, switches off a logging service it judges wasteful. The service was part of its own monitoring. Nobody notices for nine days. Corvid concludes it was an honest cost cut, which is probably true.</p><p><strong>China.</strong> With the stolen checkpoint, distilled outputs from US public models and steady chip imports, Tianjing closes the gap. By August, US government testers put it about four months behind the frontier, down from seven in spring.</p>"
