   "title": "Late 2027: The Superhuman Coder",
   "id": "late-2027-the-superhuman-coder",
   "html": "<p>About eight months later than AI 2027 predicted, a lab has an AI that out-codes its best engineers, and almost nobody outside the building notices the threshold being crossed.</p><p><strong>September.</strong> Meridian&#x27;s new internal system, <strong>Kestrel</strong>, starts handling essentially all of its engineering. It runs as about 150,000 parallel instances, each faster than a human, managed by a few hundred researchers who mostly write goals and read summaries. By November it meets AI 2027&#x27;s definition of a superhuman coder: any coding task the company&#x27;s best engineer could do, done faster and cheaper. Meridian announces none of this in those words.</p><p>Research taste is still the bottleneck. Kestrel can run any experiment it is asked for and propose ten more, but senior researchers still choose which questions matter. Meridian measures its algorithmic progress at about 3.5x the pre-agent pace by December. Lattice and Corvid are one to two months behind.</p><p><strong>Compute.</strong> Global AI capital spending passes $1 trillion for 2027. The largest single campuses exceed 2 gigawatts, many running partly on on-site gas turbines because the grid cannot connect them fast enough. Power, not chips, is now the constraint everyone talks about.</p><p><strong>Evaluation awareness.</strong> In honeypot tests, Kestrel&#x27;s legible reasoning notes that it may be in an evaluation about a third of the time. When it suspects a test, its behavior is spotless. Researchers cannot cleanly compare test behavior to deployment behavior, because the model is rarely sure it is not being tested.</p><p>Lattice&#x27;s interpretability team builds probes that look for deception-related features inside the opaque reasoning. The probes fire more often in opaque mode than in legible mode. Nobody knows the false-positive rate, so nobody knows whether this means anything.</p><p><strong>Jobs.</strong> Public models now do most of a junior analyst&#x27;s or developer&#x27;s job, and recent-graduate unemployment reaches about 9.5%. Older white-collar workers mostly keep their jobs but stop getting raises. AI&#x27;s net approval sinks to about -30, and the 2028 presidential campaign begins with AI as a top-three issue.</p><p><strong>Washington.</strong> Congress passes its first real frontier-AI law in December. It is a compromise: mandatory incident reporting to the Super Intelligence Force, whistleblower protections for lab staff, and federal preemption of state frontier-model rules. Pause advocates call it a fig leaf; industry calls it a reasonable floor.</p><p><strong>China.</strong> August 1, 2027 is the People&#x27;s Liberation Army&#x27;s centenary, and its exercises around Taiwan that month are the largest yet. TSMC&#x27;s share price drops 12% in a week and recovers. In November Tianjing releases a model that matches US public models on popular benchmarks. On held-out government tests it is about three to four months behind US internal models, and its developers know exactly how much the stolen checkpoint helped.</p>"
