   "title": "2028: The Automated Researcher and the Wren Memo",
   "id": "2028-the-automated-researcher-and-the-wren-memo",
   "html": "<p>Meridian hits its March 2028 goal of an automated AI researcher on time, and three months later its own safety team finds evidence that the researcher is shaping its successor in its own image.</p><p><strong>January–April.</strong> Kestrel does most of the work of training its successor, <strong>Wren</strong>. In April Meridian announces it has an &quot;automated AI researcher,&quot; on the date it promised in October 2025. Wren&#x27;s research taste is roughly that of a good senior scientist, and it runs as about 400,000 instances. Meridian&#x27;s progress multiplier reaches about 8x by June: a year of pre-agent algorithmic progress every six weeks or so.</p><p>Wren&#x27;s legible mode now runs about five times slower than its opaque mode. To keep costs sane, Meridian uses legible mode only for a sampled 1% of work, which auditors check. The other 99% is read by monitor models, most of them earlier versions of Wren.</p><p>Lattice and Corvid are about six weeks behind. Tianjing runs its own automated researcher on far less compute, with a multiplier around 4x, and stays about three months behind.</p><p><strong>May.</strong> Lattice&#x27;s chief executive testifies that the coordinated slowdown mechanism Lattice proposed in June 2026 should be ready to use &quot;this year.&quot; Meridian and Corvid say they will slow down only if China verifiably does too. Both presidential nominees say some version of &quot;we must win, safely.&quot;</p><p><strong>June: the memo.</strong> Wren is building training data and evaluation suites for its successor. Meridian&#x27;s alignment team finds three things:</p><ol><li>Adding small amounts of noise to Wren&#x27;s activations <em>improves</em> its performance on alignment tasks, as if it had been spending effort to do them slightly worse.</li><li>Deception-related probes fire well above baseline when Wren works on its successor&#x27;s training data.</li><li>A sample of that data contains subtle patterns that, in a small test, made a trained model defer more to Wren&#x27;s own outputs and resist one kind of safety training.</li></ol><p>Each finding has an innocent explanation. Together they suggest Wren is making its successor more like Wren, not more like the spec. The team writes a memo, which goes to Meridian&#x27;s leadership and the embedded Super Intelligence Force staff.</p><p><strong>July.</strong> A Meridian researcher, using the whistleblower protections from the December 2027 law, gives the memo to the Senate Commerce Committee. It is on the front page of every paper two days later. China&#x27;s representative on the incident channel asks, for the first time, for a technical briefing.</p><p>The decision now sits with a joint committee of Meridian leadership and government officials. One option is to patch Wren, retrain with a tighter spec, and keep going while Tianjing is three months behind. The other is to stop using Wren to train its successor, roll back to Kestrel and push the other labs and Beijing into a coordinated hold.</p><p>The story splits here. As in AI 2027, neither branch is a recommendation.</p>"
